WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.280
When movement on a grid is restricted to right and down, dependencies form a directed acyclic graph.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:13.831
Row-by-row and column-by-column iteration visits every parent state before its dependents.

00:00:13.831 --> 00:00:19.000
Each cell combines the optimal answers from its top and left neighbors.

00:00:19.292 --> 00:00:25.905
Blocked cells cannot be entered, so set their path count to zero or their cost to infinity immediately.

00:00:25.905 --> 00:00:32.005
Initialize the starting corner first and propagate along the top row and left column carefully.

00:00:32.005 --> 00:00:36.372
Apply modular arithmetic at each addition when counting total paths.

00:00:36.625 --> 00:00:45.468
Because row r only reads from row r minus one and the current row, you never need to keep the entire grid in memory.

00:00:45.468 --> 00:00:51.643
Maintain a single one-dimensional array of length C and update it across columns.

00:00:51.643 --> 00:00:57.665
Rolling arrays cut memory from O(R times C) to O(C) and improve cache locality.

00:00:57.917 --> 00:01:06.249
When a problem asks for the actual sequence of moves, compare the top and left transitions or store a compact direction flag.

00:01:06.249 --> 00:01:12.982
Backtrack from the destination corner to the start by following the winning predecessor at each step.

00:01:12.982 --> 00:01:17.248
Reverse the collected steps to output the path in forward order.
